WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.891
Authorization answers what an
authenticated identity may access or do.

2
00:00:07.971 --> 00:00:16.331
In Next.js applications, authorization may
be needed in server-rendered pages,

3
00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:22.953
API routes, route handlers, server
actions, database queries,

4
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:30.787
file access paths, administrative routes,
and service-to-service calls.

5
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:38.500
The most important rule is that sensitive
operations must verify subject,

6
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:47.062
action, resource, and context on the
server side before returning data or changing

7
00:00:47.142 --> 00:00:47.869
state.

8
00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:55.460
Object-level authorization is where many
application failures happen.

9
00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:03.295
A user may be allowed to access the
dashboard but not another user's invoice.

10
00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:09.554
A team member may belong to one
organization but not another.

11
00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:17.388
An administrator may manage users in one
tenant but not across the platform.

12
00:01:17.468 --> 00:01:20.860
Route-level checks are helpful,

13
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:27.119
but they are not enough if the code does
not verify ownership,

14
00:01:27.199 --> 00:01:33.500
tenant membership, organization
boundaries, permissions,

15
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.851
and resource-specific policy.

16
00:01:36.931 --> 00:01:42.989
Component-level display logic is not real
access control.

17
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:49.975
Hiding a link, button, tab, or page can
improve the user experience,

18
00:01:50.055 --> 00:01:56.113
but clients can be modified, requests can
be made directly,

19
00:01:56.193 --> 00:02:01.403
and APIs can be called outside the
intended screen.

20
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:09.722
The trusted enforcement point is
server-side logic close to the data and action.

21
00:02:09.802 --> 00:02:17.677
UI checks should mirror authorization
policy for usability, not replace it.

22
00:02:17.757 --> 00:02:26.723
Authorization should be consistent without
becoming detached from business context.

23
00:02:26.803 --> 00:02:31.164
Central helper functions, policy checks,

24
00:02:31.244 --> 00:02:35.606
or service layers can reduce duplication,

25
00:02:35.686 --> 00:02:41.865
but the code still needs enough context to
decide correctly.

26
00:02:41.945 --> 00:02:50.184
List filtering, detail views, mutations,
exports, invitations, role changes,

27
00:02:50.264 --> 00:02:55.474
and administrative actions each need clear
rules.

28
00:02:55.554 --> 00:02:59.189
When authorization denies access,

29
00:02:59.269 --> 00:03:07.871
the application should log enough safe
context for investigation without exposing

30
00:03:07.951 --> 00:03:10.980
sensitive data to the caller.
